---
/* The session as the interface (2026-10-23): a real capture per section. */
import { Aside } from "@astrojs/starlight/components"
import BlogPost from "../../components/landing/BlogPost.astro"
import { shot } from "../../components/landing/shots.ts"

const web = (view: string, alt: string) => shot(view, `web`, alt)
---

<BlogPost slug="session-as-interface">
  <h2 id="setup">The tracker's MCP inside the session</h2>
  <p>
    Claude Code or Codex starts with the tracker's MCP server in its config and a header that names the session (a chat is the root session of a tree). Every call the agent makes arrives as that
    session: it reads its issue, comments, links a pull request, asks a question or messages another session. The session view knows these tools and draws each result by its kind.
  </p>
  <img {...web(`composer-menu`, `The composer's + menu: the issue to implement, the team's MCP servers, computer use`)} loading="lazy" decoding="async" />

  <h2 id="cards">Tool calls drawn by kind</h2>
  <p>
    File reads, searches and commands fold into one-line trace entries; a failing test run turns red and opens onto its output. A comment written through MCP is drawn as the comment itself, and a
    code-mode script as one card with its calls grouped by tool. The engine tags every call with a kind before it reaches a client, so all four platforms draw the same card.
  </p>
  <img {...web(`trace`, `Trace lines: a command with its output, a sub-agent, a failed test run, the task list`)} loading="lazy" decoding="async" />

  <h2 id="chips">Issue references with previews</h2>
  <p>
    Any issue identifier in a message, a comment or a tool result is a chip: status glyph, identifier, title. Hover shows the assignee, status, priority and labels from data the client already has; a
    click opens the issue in the nearest view that shows it. Tool results return references as a kind and an id, and each client resolves them from its own rows.
  </p>
  <img {...web(`chat-screen`, `Issue chips in the tab strip, in the agent's text and in a child's report`)} loading="lazy" decoding="async" />

  <h2 id="images">Pictures inline in both directions</h2>
  <p>
    The agent uploads a screenshot through MCP and references it in markdown; the message, comment or plan renders it inline on every platform. When the agent reads an attachment back, the inline
    copy is capped at about 300 KB and 1280 px, because one full phone screenshot costs tens of thousands of tokens.
  </p>

  <h2 id="surfaces">The agent draws its own UI</h2>
  <p>
    A plan or a question can carry an A2UI surface: the agent describes a screen as JSON and the card paints it with the genUI renderer, native on web, iPhone, Android and the desktop app. On
    the native platforms there is no webview in between, on the web the renderer emits DOM, and what the person sets in the surface travels back with the answer.
  </p>
  <img {...web(`plan-card`, `A plan card: a picture, the agent's surface as a live preview, a choice question`)} loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
  <div class="exp-post-phones">
    <img {...shot(`plan-card`, `ios`, `The same card and surface on iPhone`)} loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
    <img {...shot(`plan-card`, `android`, `The same card and surface on Android`)} loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
  </div>

  <h2 id="results">Results carry the outcome</h2>
  <p>
    What a session wants a person to see lands in the chat's Results tab: what it showed, with its screenshots. A question or a plan waits in Needs you, and a question is answered right there. The
    pull request body is a short summary plus the result pictures of each platform; opening a PR with a UI change and no picture is refused.
  </p>
  <img {...web(`chat-results`, `A chat's Results tab: the agent's results with their screenshots`)} loading="lazy" decoding="async" />

  <h2 id="notifications">A question waits at the composer</h2>
  <p>
    When the agent asks, the question stays put as a card at the composer, with its options and a text field, and several can wait at once. The chat moves under Needs you, the inbox gets the entry,
    the phones get the push, and an @email mention notifies that person. The agent can also offer quick replies, which send on one tap.
  </p>
  <img {...web(`ask-dock`, `Waiting plans and a question at the composer`)} loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
  <img {...web(`chat-replies`, `Quick replies above the composer`)} loading="lazy" decoding="async" />

  <h2 id="bus">Sessions message each other</h2>
  <p>
    One tool, <code>sessions.send</code>, messages a session or a team agent, or starts a child. The server posts every child transition into the parent as a message, and a message is what wakes a
    session, so nothing polls. A team agent is a session with a longer life: a channel that triages, reports and starts sessions.
  </p>
  <img {...web(`agent-channel`, `A team agent's channel: its reports, a request, the session it started as one card`)} loading="lazy" decoding="async" />

  <h2 id="pattern">Four decisions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>One API for people and agents.</strong> The MCP tools are the product's own operations; the session identity rides on every call.</li>
    <li><strong>Results carry ids.</strong> Clients resolve references from local state and draw them in their own idiom.</li>
    <li><strong>Render by kind.</strong> A comment is a comment, a surface is painted natively, everything else is a trace line.</li>
    <li><strong>Messages are the only wake-up.</strong> A session runs when a message arrives and sleeps otherwise.</li>
  </ul>
  <Aside title="Try it">
    The MCP server, the session view and the renderer are open source under Apache-2.0: the <a href="/docs/build/mcp/">MCP docs</a>, the <a href="/docs/using/sessions/">sessions docs</a> and <a
      href="/sdk/genui/">exponential.at/sdk/genui</a>.
  </Aside>
</BlogPost>
